# Photos you are willing to lose -- and lose means lose: the photo service removes
# items outright, they do NOT go to Recently Deleted. The copy in your destination
# folder is the only one that will be left.
#
# One entry per line. Copy this file to sacrificial.txt, which is git-ignored.
#
# THE EASY WAY -- the local path, exactly as you see it in the Finder, relative to
# your destination folder:
#
#   2026/09/IMG_7045.JPG
#
# It is unique inside a destination, and you can check it with your own eyes before
# committing a photo to deletion. That is the point.
#
# Also accepted:
#
#   /DCIM/117APPLE/IMG_7045.JPG    the device path -- exact, but you have to look it up
#   IMG_7045.JPG                   a bare name, only when it is unambiguous
#
# Bare names are a convenience with a trap: file names on an iPhone are NOT unique.
# iOS numbers photos per folder and starts again in the next one, so IMG_7045.JPG
# can be a photo from last year *and* the throwaway you took a minute ago. If a name
# matches two imported files the run stops and prints both rather than guessing.
#
# Naming a file selects the whole *item* it belongs to: the still, its Live Photo
# half and its .AAE sidecar go together, because that is what iOS deletes. At most
# three items per run, and that ceiling is in the code, not here.
#
# Nothing outside this list can be deleted by the test suite. There is no
# "most recent N" here and no default -- if this file is missing or empty, the
# destructive test skips.
#
# Make the photo on purpose (MANUAL_TESTING.md check 16b): Camera with Live Photos
# on, one throwaway shot, then edit it in Photos so it gets a sidecar too.

# 2026/09/IMG_7045.JPG
